  "ExperimentDesign": "To evaluate our proposed method, we applied the simulation guidelines proposed by Kellner et al.53 We particularly select these simulation techniques as they are commonly used techniques in evaluating SPLs.54–56 We adopt the Betty FM Generator (http://www.isa. us.es/betty/) to randomly generate feature models with different sizes and constraints. In addition, since the structure of both feature models and goal models are similar, we also generate goal models using the Betty generator. To empirically evaluate our proposed configuration process, we designed various scenarios in which feature models with different sizes (eg, different numbers of features and different ratios of structural constraints) are generated. Moreover, for each feature model, we generated a random goal model including equal ratios of goal model structural variabilities. Table 14 summarizes the various scenarios and the detailed characteristics of the elements (feature and goal mod- els) that have been used in experimentation. According to the anal- ysis reported in another work,46 conducted on the feature models collected on the SPLOT repository,49 the average number of integrity constraints for feature models is 18%. Therefore, for the purpose of our experiments, we developed feature models with a 20% integrity constraint ratio. To evaluate the execution time of the developed optimization model, we consider 4 independent variables (number of features, number of goals, ratio of feature model structural constraints, and ratio of goal model structural variability) and 1 dependent variable, called time. The devel- oped optimization model consists of 2 parts. The first part is an objec- tive function, which is directly influenced by the number of available featuresandtheirutilityvalues.Inthisstudy,eachfeaturewasassigned with a utility value produced by a random function with normal distri- bution. The second part is devoted to the linear constraints so that the feature and goal models’ structures are transformed into linear con- straints using the developed transformation rules. To consider the map- ping links, we connected the generated feature models’ leaf features to the leaf elements in the goal models. We use an ILP solver named IBM CPLEX (http://www.ilog.com/products/cplex/), which implements the simplexmethod57 tosolvetheprovidedILPmodels.Theevaluationwas performed on a dedicated 3.20-GHz Core i5 personal computer with 8 GBofmemory,runningaWindows8operatingsystemalongwithJava Runtime Environment v1.8.0. Note that the experiments were repeated 25 times to remove any effect of randomness. In addition, for the development of a configura- tion problem instance, in each case, the generated feature model was integrated with 50 goal models. Hence, for each configuration problem, we developed 50 instances, and for each instance, we ran the problem 25 times."
